<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miriam Makeba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/2008">2008</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Zenzile Miriam Makeba, Mama Africa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miriam Makeba, nicknamed Mama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, was a South African singer, songwriter, actress and civil rights activist who campaigned against apartheid. Born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, she sang with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan">Manhattan</a> Brothers and the Skylarks in the 1950s, and a role in the anti-apartheid film Come Back, Africa (1959) took her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where Harry Belafonte became her mentor, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In 1960 her passport was cancelled when she tried to return for her mother's funeral, and she remained in exile until 1990. She recorded &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pata-pata">Pata Pata</a>&quot; (1967), shared a Grammy with Belafonte and testified against the South African government at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>; she died of a heart attack at a concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miriam-makeba.jpg" alt="Miriam Makeba" width="330" height="580" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reprise Records · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miriam_Makeba_(1968).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zenzile Miriam Makeba was born on 4 March 1932 in the Black township of Prospect near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>. Her Xhosa father, Caswell Makeba, was a teacher who died when she was six. Her Swazi mother, Christina, was a domestic worker. The name Zenzile comes from the Xhosa words her grandmother muttered after a difficult birth, which mean 'you brought this on yourself'.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Makeba began with the Cuban Brothers, a close-harmony group, and at 21 joined the Manhattan Brothers, the only woman among them. With them she recorded her first hit in 1953. In 1956 she joined the all-woman Skylarks, who blended jazz with traditional South African melodies, though she received no royalties from this work. These groups performed mbube, a style of vocal harmony drawing on jazz, ragtime, Anglican hymns and indigenous music.</p><p>She and Harry Belafonte received a Grammy Award for Best Folk Recording for An Evening with Belafonte/Makeba (1965). After she married Stokely Carmichael in March 1968, her popularity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> fell sharply and the government took an interest in her. While she was in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahamas">Bahamas</a> she was refused a visa to return, and the couple moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>; she did not go back to the United States until 1987. Her daughter Bongi died in 1985, and Makeba moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. In 1986 she joined Paul Simon's Graceland tour, which caused controversy because the album had been recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, in breach of a cultural boycott that she herself endorsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>'s release in February 1990, Makeba entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> on a six-day visa on 10 June 1990. In 1991 she recorded Eyes on Tomorrow with Dizzy Gillespie and Nina Simone, and in 1992 she played the mother in the film Sarafina! She died of a heart attack during a concert in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 9 November 2008.</p><p>Makeba released more than 30 albums and was among the first African musicians to gain worldwide recognition. She said that what she sang was not politics but the truth, and she sang at independence ceremonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, Tanganyika and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>. On her death Mandela said that her music inspired a powerful sense of hope in all of us.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miriam Makeba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charles Worrod re-recorded it at Equator Sound Studios and marketed it until it became known internationally, and <b>Miriam Makeba</b>'s early recording, which added a third verse, made it famous across Africa and then the world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malaika">Malaika</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miriam Makeba</b>, who carried many protest songs of the period inside and outside South Africa, made &quot;Meadowlands&quot; widely known, and it became an anthem of the movement against apartheid.</blockquote><a href="/songs/meadowlands">Meadowlands</a></li><li><blockquote>It came ahead of Brenda Fassie's &quot;Vuli Ndlela&quot;, Fela Kuti's &quot;Lady&quot;, Franco's &quot;Mario&quot; and <b>Miriam Makeba</b>'s version of &quot;Malaika&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sweet-mother">Sweet Mother</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Miriam Makeba</b> sang &quot;Mbube&quot; at President John F. Kennedy's birthday in 1962.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-lion-sleeps-tonight">The Lion Sleeps Tonight</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in Castel Volturno, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Johannesburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5290 3126 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Miriam Makeba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5740 3396h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5740 3396h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-26.2,28.04&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
